1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:07,683
Claude is great with words. But on its own, it can't check your database, call your API, or look up a customer's order.

2
00:00:08,133 --> 00:00:16,994
Tools fix that. In this video, you'll learn how tools work, the full tool use lifecycle with stop reasons, and how the Tool Runner handles the loop for you.

3
00:00:17,444 --> 00:00:20,732
We'll build a real example, step by step, in Python.

4
00:00:22,282 --> 00:00:23,117
So, what is a tool?

5
00:00:23,567 --> 00:00:32,919
A tool is a function you describe to Claude, with three parts: a name, a description, and an input schema, which is a JSON schema for its parameters.

6
00:00:33,369 --> 00:00:38,201
Claude reads that description, and decides when to call the tool, and with which inputs.

7
00:00:38,651 --> 00:00:46,659
But here's the key idea: Claude never runs anything itself. It asks. Your code runs the function, and sends the result back.

8
00:00:47,109 --> 00:00:52,822
Some tools, like web search, run on Anthropic's servers instead. Today: your own tools.

9
00:00:54,272 --> 00:00:58,024
Here's the tool use lifecycle. Every tool call is a round trip.

10
00:00:58,474 --> 00:01:01,793
One: you send the conversation, plus your list of tools.

11
00:01:02,243 --> 00:01:12,534
Two: if Claude wants a tool, the response comes back with stop reason, tool use. It contains a tool use block, with an id, the tool name, and the input.

12
00:01:12,984 --> 00:01:21,309
Three: you run the tool, and send back a tool result, with the same id. Then Claude writes its answer, with stop reason, end turn.

13
00:01:21,759 --> 00:01:28,344
Claude may call tools several times in a row. So the rule is simple: keep looping while the stop reason is tool use.

14
00:01:29,794 --> 00:01:36,383
Let's look closer. On the left, Claude's request: the tool name, the input it chose, and a unique id.

15
00:01:36,833 --> 00:01:45,949
On the right, your answer, sent as a user message. The tool use id links the result to the request. And is error tells Claude if the tool failed.

16
00:01:46,399 --> 00:01:53,486
Two rules: the tool result must come right after Claude's tool use message, and tool results go first in the message content.

17
00:01:54,936 --> 00:01:55,992
When should you use a tool?

18
00:01:56,442 --> 00:02:05,670
When Claude needs to do something, like send or update. When it needs fresh or private data, like your orders. Or when you need a guaranteed JSON shape.

19
00:02:06,120 --> 00:02:13,631
Skip tools when Claude can answer from text alone, like a summary or a translation. Every tool call is an extra round trip.

20
00:02:15,081 --> 00:02:18,581
Our use case: an order status assistant for a small online shop.

21
00:02:19,031 --> 00:02:23,128
Without a tool, Claude can't see the orders. It can only apologize.

22
00:02:23,578 --> 00:02:30,123
With a get order status tool, Claude asks for the data, our code reads it, and the answer is based on real data.

23
00:02:31,573 --> 00:02:36,767
Here's the project: the orders in a JSON file, and a small function that looks one up.

24
00:02:37,217 --> 00:02:42,145
If the order doesn't exist, it raises an error with a clear message. We'll come back to that.

25
00:02:42,595 --> 00:02:45,953
First, I ask Claude about an order, with no tools at all.

26
00:02:46,403 --> 00:02:51,680
Stop reason: end turn. Claude finished, but it simply can't know. Let's give it a tool.

27
00:02:52,630 --> 00:02:54,335
Step one: define the tool.

28
00:02:54,785 --> 00:03:02,365
I import the SDK and my function, then describe the tool in a list: a name, a description, and an input schema.

29
00:03:05,215 --> 00:03:14,447
The description matters most. Claude reads it to decide when to call the tool. The docs recommend three or four sentences: what it does, and when to use it.

30
00:03:14,897 --> 00:03:19,280
The input schema says the tool takes one required string: the order id.

31
00:03:20,130 --> 00:03:22,314
Step two: run the lifecycle loop.

32
00:03:22,764 --> 00:03:30,650
Inside a while loop, I call the API with the tools, and print the stop reason. If it's not tool use, Claude is done, so I break.

33
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:41,751
Otherwise, I append Claude's message, run each tool use block, and send back a tool result with the same id. Then the loop calls Claude again.

34
00:03:42,201 --> 00:03:44,873
Now, let's run it, and watch the stop reasons.

35
00:03:45,323 --> 00:03:54,683
There's the lifecycle: first tool use, my code runs the function, then end turn, with an answer based on real data. Claude's wording will differ in your run.

36
00:03:55,133 --> 00:04:02,014
Step three: the Tool Runner. That loop works, but you'd write it in every project. The SDK can do it for you.

37
00:04:02,464 --> 00:04:10,404
With the beta tool decorator, my plain Python function becomes a tool. The SDK builds the schema from the type hints and the docstring.

38
00:04:10,854 --> 00:04:19,970
Then the tool runner, which is in beta in the official SDKs. It runs the tools, appends the messages, and loops until Claude stops calling tools.

39
00:04:20,420 --> 00:04:26,343
Max iterations puts a safety limit on the loop. And I still get each message, to print its stop reason.

40
00:04:27,193 --> 00:04:36,876
Step four: handle errors. This time, I ask about two orders: ten forty-two, and nine thousand nine hundred ninety-nine, which doesn't exist.

41
00:04:37,326 --> 00:04:43,214
Claude calls the tool twice, in one turn. For nine nine nine nine, my function raises an error.

42
00:04:43,664 --> 00:04:50,329
The Tool Runner catches it, and sends it to Claude as a tool result with is error set to true. The script doesn't crash.

43
00:04:50,779 --> 00:04:57,717
And because my error message says what to do next, Claude answers the first order, and asks the customer to check the second number.

44
00:04:58,167 --> 00:05:03,001
In the manual loop, you'd catch the exception yourself, and set is error in the tool result.

45
00:05:04,551 --> 00:05:09,883
Step five: know every stop reason, because your code should check it on every response.

46
00:05:10,333 --> 00:05:13,067
End turn: Claude finished. Use the answer.

47
00:05:13,517 --> 00:05:16,714
Tool use: run the tool, and send the result back.

48
00:05:17,164 --> 00:05:24,182
Max tokens: the answer hit your max tokens limit, so it's cut off. Raise the limit, or continue the response.

49
00:05:24,632 --> 00:05:28,198
Stop sequence: Claude wrote one of your custom stop sequences.

50
00:05:28,648 --> 00:05:35,931
Pause turn: a server tool, like web search, hit its loop limit. Send the conversation back, and Claude continues.

51
00:05:36,381 --> 00:05:45,707
Refusal: Claude declined to respond. Check the stop details. There's also model context window exceeded, when the response fills the context window.

52
00:05:47,257 --> 00:05:48,455
Four tips before you ship.

53
00:05:48,905 --> 00:05:54,235
One: write detailed descriptions. It's the biggest factor in how well Claude uses your tools.

54
00:05:54,685 --> 00:06:00,937
Two: write helpful error messages. Say what went wrong, and what to try next, so Claude can recover.

55
00:06:01,387 --> 00:06:06,640
Three: always bound the loop, with max iterations, or a counter in your own loop.

56
00:06:07,090 --> 00:06:16,839
And four: the Tool Runner is the easy default. When you need human approval before a tool runs, custom logging, or conditional execution, use the manual loop.

57
00:06:18,389 --> 00:06:19,042
Let's recap.

58
00:06:19,492 --> 00:06:25,416
A tool is a name, a description, and an input schema. Claude asks, and your code runs it.

59
00:06:25,866 --> 00:06:31,616
The lifecycle: loop while the stop reason is tool use, and send back a tool result with the same id.

60
00:06:32,066 --> 00:06:36,925
And the Tool Runner runs that loop for you, and turns your exceptions into is error results.

61
00:06:38,375 --> 00:06:45,249
All the code, an offline test you can run without an API key, and a stop reason cheat sheet are linked in the description.

62
00:06:45,699 --> 00:06:52,530
If this helped, like the video, and subscribe to Zero Cost AI for more free AI tutorials. See you in the next one!

